To ensure the time picker automatically defaults to a perfectly valid time (and gives the user a buffer if they take a minute or two to click submit), we should pre-fill the state with **Current Time + 35 minutes** whenever they open the action screen or toggle the "Override Schedule" option.
This prevents the picker from starting out blank or accidentally causing a validation failure if they sit on the screen for too long.
Here are the exact 3 replacements to make in **VoucherAuthorization.js**:
### 1. Pre-fill the Universal Action Screen (handleProceed)
Find your handleProceed method (around line 450) and update the else block to inject a safe default time.
**Replace the entire handleProceed method with this:**
```javascript
  const handleProceed = () => {
    setIsActionMode(true); // Open the universal action screen

    // Only pre-fill time if it's the final approval
    if (viewingBatch?.isFinalApproval === "Y") {
      if (vidDetails?.SCHEDULED_TIME) {
        const dateObj = new Date(vidDetails.SCHEDULED_TIME);
        dateObj.setMinutes(dateObj.getMinutes() - dateObj.getTimezoneOffset());
        setFinalScheduleTime(dateObj.toISOString().slice(0, 16));
      } else {
        // FIX: Automatically pre-fill with a valid safe time (Current Time + 35 mins)
        setFinalScheduleTime(dayjs().add(35, 'minute').format("YYYY-MM-DDTHH:mm"));
      }
    }
  };

```
### 2. Auto-refresh when clicking "Override Schedule"
If the user clicks "Keep Original Routing" and then waits 10 minutes before clicking "Override Schedule" again, the previously stored time might now be invalid. We will add logic to auto-refresh the time to a valid +35 minutes when they click the Override card.
Find the <Box onClick={() => setScheduleOverrideType("OVERRIDE")} inside the routing options (around line 1240) and replace that **entire <Grid item>** with this:
